          "worked": "Write $x=6^y$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}6^{-2}&=\\frac{1}{36}\\\\6^{-1}&=\\frac{1}{6}\\\\6^0&=1\\\\6^1&=6\\\\6^2&=36\\end{align*}$$\n\nPlot $(\\frac{1}{36},-2)$, $(\\frac{1}{6},-1)$, $(1,0)$, $(6,1)$ and $(36,2)$. Join them with a smooth increasing logarithmic curve. The curve approaches the $y$-axis from the right as $y$ decreases.",
